.he "date (1)                     8/16/26                     date (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
date
- print and set the date
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
date [ -u ] [ yymmddhhmm [ .ss ] ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
With no argument, date prints the current date and time.  With an
argument it sets the clock, which only the super-user may do.
.sp
The argument is yymmddhhmm: two digits each of year, month, day, hour
and minute, with an optional .ss for seconds.  The hour is on the 24
hour clock.  The year, month and day may be left off, and then the
current ones are used.  For example, date 8506131627 sets the clock
to June 13 1985 at 4:27 PM.
.sp
The kernel keeps Greenwich mean time.  An argument is taken to be
local time, and date adds the correction before it stores it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-u	Print universal time, or take the argument as universal time rather than local.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The Berkeley date had flags to set the kernel's daylight-saving and
timezone values, and to talk to timed over the network.  Micronix has
no such kernel state and no network, so only -u remains; the timezone
is a single minutes-west figure in libc.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sh (1)
